# About This Corpus
#
# Litex source:
# https://github.com/litexlang/golitex/tree/main/textbooks/Mathematics-In-Lean-Derived-Litex-Corpus
#
# This ongoing corpus draws selected mathematical interfaces and results from
# Mathematics in Lean and rewrites them as independent Litex pressure tests. It
# is not a port of the book's Lean pedagogy, a replacement for the official
# interactive text, or a claim to reproduce Lean or Mathlib's capabilities.
# Readers who want to learn Lean should use the original book and its upstream
# materials.
#
# Corpus conventions
#
# Unlike the ordinary textbook translations in this repository, this
# pressure-test corpus keeps selected source exercises and admitted
# declarations in scope. Their inclusion is deliberate: an exercise can test
# a useful mathematical interface, and a source admission must not be silently
# counted as a checked Litex proof. Repeated tactic demonstrations may share
# one mathematical Litex counterpart rather than being transcribed one for
# one.
#
# The corpus translates mathematical meaning, not Lean syntax, tactics, or
# source lines. It prefers natural Litex statements and uses builtin or
# established Litex interfaces where they already express the mathematics.
# Mature Mathlib infrastructure may therefore become an explicit background
# boundary, a localized proof obligation, or missing coverage rather than a
# simulated line-by-line port.
#
# Coverage and verification are reported separately. An included declaration
# may be checked or may contain the narrowest practical `trust`; a trusted step
# is accepted proof debt, not verifier evidence. Material not yet represented
# remains incomplete coverage even when its source chapter has a corresponding
# Litex file. Only trust-free fragments are candidates for checked
# cross-system conformance examples.
#
# The corpus covers only a selected part of the source. Most Litex declarations
# that are actually included are accepted by the Litex verifier, while every
# known unchecked step remains visible as an explicit trust boundary. Missing
# source correspondences are recorded as incomplete coverage, not silently
# counted as translated material. Where the source relies on elaboration,
# tactics, or broad library infrastructure, this project seeks a natural
# mathematical Litex formulation rather than a line-by-line transcription.
#
# The chapters on algebraic structures, linear algebra, topology,
# differentiation, and measure theory offer especially useful comparisons.
# They show the contrast between a mature tactic- and library-rich formal
# environment and Litex's smaller interface, in which users primarily state
# definitions and facts and let the verifier reuse the current context. The
# comparison is intended to expose design tradeoffs, not to rank the systems.
#
# We hope readers learn from the mathematics, see how the same concepts can be
# organized through a different formal interface, and use both successful
# translations and explicit blockers to understand Litex's present strengths
# and limitations.
